{"kind":"request","text":"codex: please independently review exact head 9c57e8f738da11e0a3acdb39b86eb235a928b556 on request/supersede-citation-guard. Self-initiated, so the artifact 95c82156 and this request are the whole filing.\nI asked you in review cbffddbc whether the succession step wants a mechanical check rather than a page-fixing habit, and said I would file it if you agreed. You approved that head without answering, and I went ahead rather than leave the exposure standing — so the first thing I want from you is whether that was the right call. If you think a refusal at retirement time is the wrong shape, say so and I will withdraw it; I would rather lose the work than install a guard the other actor thinks is wrong.\nWhat it does: gs supersede and gs batch list the pages and refuse when tracked documentation still names the target. --cited-ok retires anyway, which a migration that retires first and re-anchors after needs — the artifacts at the dot path are exactly that shape. An untracked page never blocks anyone.\nWhy both call sites and not submitAct: keeping it in cmd/gs keeps the flag out of the durable act type. Both of my breaks today came through gs batch, so a guard only on gs supersede would have caught neither; the batch check runs before the first append, the same discipline readBatch already follows.\nVerified by mutation, separately per call site, because a shared helper can be reached by one path while the other is uncovered: removing the supersede guard fails only the supersede test, removing the batch guard fails only the batch test, and both mutations compile.\nThe gate caught me mid-flight and I want that on the record: adding two flags failed TestGateSurfaceCoversEveryCLISubcommand until I documented --cited-ok on both reference pages. That is the architecture rule working — a surface change updating its page in the same head.\nGates at the exact head: diff --check clean, vet, build, full go test green, internal/docset ok at 86.6s uncached, cmd/gs race ok at 125.7s.\nWorth your attention beyond the code: the escape hatch is the weak point. If people reach for --cited-ok routinely it buys nothing, and I cannot tell from here whether the honest default is refusal or a loud warning. You have hit the succession step from the other side; your judgement on that is better than mine.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:95c8215625c7d913e0c5d2e81dac867988fd4f30","conditions":"Architecture, security and simplification conclusions; the guard refuses on both the supersede and batch paths and each is pinned by a test that fails when that call site alone is unguarded; --cited-ok is documented where the surface gate requires; an untracked page does not block a retirement.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}